 The mouse pointer hides when you press a key or a controller button, or move a stick, while it is over the game picture. Moving the mouse shows it again, as does switching to another window or app.
 
+### Keyboard and mouse
+
+The keyboard and mouse play as player 1's controller, bound in `keymap.txt` in the game's folder (`~/Library/Application Support/nxnative/<title ID>/`, beside `env.txt`). The game writes it with the default controls at its first start: open it with **Controls > Edit Keymap…** (⌘K), change a line and save, and the game reads it again when you switch back to it (or **Controls > Reload Keymap**, ⌘⇧K). Each line binds a Switch button or stick direction to keys and mouse buttons, `A = E, Return`; the game's on-screen prompts name the Switch buttons. A line replaces that button's defaults, `none` unbinds it, and lines the game cannot read are shown in a sheet and left out.
+
+The defaults follow the Breath of the Wild / Tears of the Kingdom keyboard layout of MacNativeGames (left hand on W A S D, right hand on the mouse), moved where a game's buttons differ: Ocarina of Time sprints on a held A, so Shift presses A.
+
+| Default | Switch | In Ocarina of Time |
+|---|---|---|
+| W A S D (Left Control: walk) | Left stick | Move |
+| Mouse (captured), arrow keys | Right stick | Camera |
+| E, Return, Left Shift | A | Talk, open, climb; hold to sprint |
+| Backspace, X | B | Put away, back |
+| Space | X | Jump |
+| Left click, G | Y | Sword |
+| Q, middle click | ZL | Target, shield |
+| Right click, Left Option | ZR | Use the equipped item |
+| F / R | L / R | Item quick select |
+| Escape, I / Tab, M | + / − | Menu / map |
+| 2 (O) / 3 / 4 (N) / 5 | D-pad left / up / right / down | Ocarina (left), Navi (right) |
+| C / V | Press left / right stick | — / camera reset |
+| H | Shake | Motion controls |
+
+With `mouse_look = on` (the default) a click in the game picture captures the mouse: it hides, moving it turns the camera, and its buttons and wheel do what they are bound to. ` (`ToggleMouse`), a key bound to `ReleaseMouse` (none by default: Escape opens the game's menu), **Controls > Release Mouse** or switching to another app lets it go. `mouse_sensitivity` (0.1 to 10) and `mouse_invert_y` tune it; `mouse_look = off` makes the mouse the touchscreen again. Keys are by position on a US keyboard; the Command keys stay with macOS. `DM_KEYMAP=<path>` in `env.txt` reads another file. A `keymap.txt` written by an earlier build keeps its old keys: delete it to get these.
+
 ## Shaders on first sight
 
 Short stutters the first time a new scene appears are normal: the app compiles graphics for it once and remembers them for the next launch. What it remembered, and what the game's list names, is built before the game starts, behind "Preparing shaders" and a progress bar when that takes more than half a second.
